package cajeta.net.ws;

import cajeta.net.NetException;
import cajeta.lang.String;

/**
 * Intermediate root of the **WebSocket (RFC 6455) family** under
 * [`cajeta.net.NetException`](../NetException.cajeta) — the WS peer of
 * the HTTP layer's [`HttpException`](../http/HttpException.cajeta). The
 * [`NetException`](../NetException.cajeta) design note names this level
 * explicitly: *"the WebSocket family under a `WebSocketException`"*.
 *
 * Every WebSocket protocol fault descends from this one type so a WS
 * façade (`WebSocket.connect`, a server `upgrade`) — and any
 * request-boundary handler — can declare or catch **one**
 * `throws WebSocketException` / `catch (WebSocketException e)` that
 * covers every WS-level fault, distinct from a transport-level
 * [`NetException`](../NetException.cajeta) (a socket reset, a DNS
 * failure), while a `catch (NetException e)` still nets *both*. The full
 * hierarchy lands in **NET-10.8** (`HandshakeRejected`,
 * `MessageTooLarge`, `ConnectionClosed` carrying the close code); the
 * frame codec (**NET-10.3**) needs only this root plus the
 * [`ProtocolViolationException`](ProtocolViolationException.cajeta) leaf
 * it raises on a malformed frame, so those two land here and NET-10.8
 * fills in the rest.
 *
 * ## The inherited `kind`
 *
 * As a [`NetException`](../NetException.cajeta), every instance carries
 * the cross-platform [`kind`](../NetException.cajeta) ordinal. A WS
 * protocol fault is an **invalid-input** fault (the peer sent bytes that
 * violate RFC 6455), not an errno-derived socket error, so — like the
 * HTTP and URI families — it classifies to
 * [`NetException.KIND_INVALID`](../NetException.cajeta) (12). A single
 * `catch (NetException e)` can therefore branch on `e.kind == 12` for
 * "the peer sent garbage" across URI, JSON, HTTP, and WebSocket alike.
 *
 * ## Construction
 *
 * Like every `cajeta.net` subtype (and per
 * [`cajeta.error.Throwable`](../../error/Throwable.cajeta)'s contract,
 * "each subclass repeats the field assignment"), the concrete leaves do
 * **not** chain through a `super(...)` call — each sets the inherited
 * `message`/`cause`/`kind` fields directly. This `(message)` constructor
 * exists for the rare case a caller raises a bare `WebSocketException`;
 * it stamps `kind = NetException.KIND_INVALID`.
 */
public class WebSocketException extends NetException {
    public WebSocketException(String message) {
        this.message = message;
        this.cause = 0;
        this.kind = 12;   // NetException.KIND_INVALID
    }
}
